Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME NORMAL TEMPARARTURE OF LEMON CROPS?,Normal Temperature- 20-25 degree,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME HARVESTING TEMPARARTURE OF LEMON CROPS?,Harvesting Temperature- 25-30 degree,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME RAINFALL OF LEMON CROPS?,Rainfall- 75-200cm,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME SUITABLE TIME OF  LEMON CROP?,The best season for planting is July-August.,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME SOIL MANAGEMENT OF  LEMON CROP?,Lemons can be grown in all types of soils. Light soils having good drainage are suitable for its cultivation. PH range of soil should be 5.5-7.5. They can also grow in slightly alkaline and acidic soils. Light loam well drained soils are best for lemon cultivation.,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME FIELD PREPARATION OF  LEMON CROP?,"Land should be ploughed, cross ploughed and leveled properly. Planting is done on terraces against slopes in hilly areas. High density planting is also possible in such areas.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME SPACING OF  LEMON CROP?,Spacing:Spacing between plants should be kept between 4.5.5. Pits of size 6000cm should be dug for planting seedlings. 10Kg of Farmyard Manure and 500g of single superphosphate should be applied to pits while planting.,"[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME SOWING DEPTH OF  LEMON CROP?,"Sowing Depth:Pits of size 6000cm should be dug for planting seedlings.
","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME HARVESTING OF  LEMON CROP?,"On attaining proper size, shape along with attractive color having TSS to Acid ratio of 12:1, kinnow fruits is ready for harvest. Depending upon variety fruits are generally ready for harvesting in Mid- January to Mid- February. Do harvesting at proper time as too early or too late harvesting will give poor quality.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME POST HARVESTING OF  LEMON CROP?,"After harvesting, wash fruits with clean water then dip fruits in Chlorinated water@2.5ml per Liter water and then partially dried them. To improve appearance along with to maintain good quality, do Citrashine wax coating along with foam. Then these fruits are dried under shade and then packing is done. Fruits are packed in boxes","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Cultural Practices,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME METHOD OF SOWING OF  LEMON CROP?,"Method of sowing:Propagation--Plants are propagated by budding or air layering.

","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Water Management,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME WATER MANAGEMENT OF  LEMON CROP?,"Lemon requires irrigation at regular intervals. Lifesaving irrigation should be given in winters and summers. Irrigation is necessary for Flowering, Fruiting and proper plant growth. Over irrigation may also leads to diseases like Root rot and collar rot. High frequency irrigation is beneficial. Salty water is injurious for crop plants. Partial drying out of soil in spring may not affect plants.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Weed Management,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME WEED MANAGEMENT OF  LEMON CROP?,"Weed can be controlled by hand-hoeing and also controlled by chemically, use glyphosate@1.6litre per 150 litre of water. Use glyphosate only on weeds not on crop plants.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Fertilizer Uses,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME BETTER/GOOD/FERTILIZER USE S OF  LEMON CROP?,"When age of crop is 1-3year, apply well decomposed cow dung@5-20kg per Tree and Urea@100-300gm per Tree. For 4-6year old crop, apply well decomposed cowdung@25-50kg and Urea@100-300gm per Tree. For 7-9year old crop, apply Urea@600-800gm per tree and well decomposed cow dung@60-90kg per Tree. When crop is 10year old or above, apply cowdung@100kg or Urea@800-1600gm per tree.Apply whole amount of cow dung during December month whereas apply Urea in two parts; apply first of Urea in February, and second dose in April-May month. At time of applying first dose of Urea, apply whole dose of SSP fertilizer.If fruit drop is observed, to control excessive fruit drop, take spray of 2,4-D@10gm in 500Ltr of water. Take first spray in March end, then in April end. Repeat the spray in August and September end. If cotton is planted in nearby field of citrus, avoid spraying of 2,4-D, instead take spray of GA3.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Varieties,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME LEMON VARIETIES?,"Punjab Baramasi: Shoots dropping are usually ground touching. Lemon has yellow fruits, round shaped with tapering base. Seedless fruits and are juicy in nature. The average fruit yield is 84 kg per tree.
Eureka: Semi-vigorous tree. Lemon-yellow skin color, juice is strongly acidic having excellent flavor. Fruit ripens in the month of August.
Punjab Galgal: Vigorous trees with light green foliage color. Medium size, oval shaped fruit. Juice is very acidic with 8-10 seeds per fruit. Fruits mature in the months of November-December. The average fruit yield is 80-100 kg per tree.
PAU Baramasi: The right time of fruit maturing is first week of July. It contains very less amount of seeds. It gives an average yield of 84kg per tree. 
PAU Baramasi-1: The right time of fruit maturing is last week of November month. The fruit is seedless. It gives an average yield of 80kg per tree. 
Other States Varieties:
Rasraj: Developed by IIHR. Yellow colored fruits content 70% juice and 12 seeds. Its acidity is 6% and TSS content is about 8 brix. It is resistant to bacterial blight and canker disease.
Lisbon lemon: It is resistant to frost and high wind velocity. Fruit are of medium size, having lemon yellow color with smooth surface.
Lucknow seedless: Fruits are of medium size with yellow color.
Pant Lemon: Dwarf variety having medium size juicy fruits. It is resistant to scab, canker and gummosis.
Besides, Assam Lemon, Italian Lemon, Eureka lemon, Malta lemon also famous varieties in lemon.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
Fruits,Lemon,Varieties,INFORMATION REGARDING/DETAILS ABOUT/TELL ME LEMON VARIETIEY PUNJAB BARAMASI?,"Punjab Baramasi: Shoots dropping are usually ground touching. Lemon has yellow fruits, round shaped with tapering base. Seedless fruits and are juicy in nature. The average fruit yield is 84 kg per tree.","[Reference: www.apnikheti.com,  Punjab Agricultural University Ludhiana, Department of Agriculture, Indian Agricultural Research Institute New Delhi,Indian Institute of Wheat and Barley Research, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare]"
